  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining breezy through the rest of the weekend\nwith temperatures moderating some for Sunday. \n\nFor tonight, the winds will remain elevated as the pressure \ngradient persists but they will diminish a bit compared to\nearlier today, especially in terms of gustiness. The winds \nalong with some cloud cover moving through overnight will help \nkeep temperatures from really bottoming out. Generally expect \nlows in the low to mid 40s. \n\nFor Sunday, temperatures warm a few degrees compared to today with \nhighs mostly in the low 60s. Some morning clouds will give way to \nafternoon sun. Expect west winds generally around 10 to 15 gusting \nat times to around 20 mph due to a pressure gradient persisting \nbetween low pressure over eastern Canada and high pressure to our \nsouth and west.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A potentially strong coastal storm/nor'easter late \nnext week.\n\nA significant upper-level trough is forecast to evolve across the \neastern U.S. later next week. This should support a surface low that \ninitially tracks near the Ohio Valley, however a secondary low \nshould develop in the vicinity of the northern Mid-Atlantic. The \nmodel guidance overall is in decent agreement with the development \nof the strong upper-level trough, however there are some differences \non the handling of the embedded shortwaves and thus the surface low. \nThe timing and amplification of the trough will also have an impact \non the secondary surface low development and track. This will be \nimportant as it will determine where the main forcing for ascent \nbecomes established along with colder air on the west/northwest side \nof the developing coastal storm/nor'easter.\n\nThe strength of the parent upper-level trough would result in a \ncolder air mass moving in with time. This could be sufficiently cold \nenough for a rain/snow mix of even some snow across our far western \nto northwestern zones. This would have a greater chance of \noccurrence if the secondary low develops and takes over instead of \nthe initial low remaining primary and tracking over our area or to \nour west. Elsewhere, a decent amount of rain is possible. The amount \nof precipitation (rain and/or snow), and precipitation types for at \nleast portions of our area, will depend on the ultimate development \nand track of the secondary surface low and the associated thermal \nprofiles that evolve across our region. The coastal impacts, \nincluding the potential for stronger winds and also tidal flooding, \nare less certain because if the surface low tracks over our area \nthat would lessen the residence time of the (stronger) onshore flow. \nLots of uncertainty with the details this far out in time (Thursday \nnight through Friday night), therefore this time frame will continue \nto be monitored.",
